            'A digital-first file can use a live blue link and a slightly tighter line height. A print-first file must survive greyscale, cheap toner, and a hole punch. Light grey body text that looks elegant on a retina screen disappears on a municipal printer. White text on a navy header becomes an empty band. If your standing talent-pool CV might be printed in an Istanbul office and also parsed in a German ATS, design for the worse of the two: dark text, white ground, selectable type, A4.',
            'Bleed, crop marks, and designer slugs belong in a print shop, not in a CV. Spreads (facing pages) belong in a book. Form fields and comment annotations confuse some parsers; flatten comments before upload if you used them while drafting. Digital signatures and certificates on the PDF itself are unnecessary for an application and can block some tools from opening the file. Save as a standard PDF, not as a PDF/X poster and not as a Word file with a .pdf typed on the end.',
